-- ScholarMesh -- 08a -- seed notes: Database Management Systems + Software Engineering
INSERT INTO DOCUMENT (title, description, file_path, file_type, file_size_bytes, visibility, content, student_id, course_id, upload_date)
SELECT v.title, v.description, v.file_path, 'md', octet_length(v.content), v.visibility, v.content,
       s.student_id, c.course_id, CURRENT_TIMESTAMP - v.age
FROM (VALUES
  ('Normalization from 1NF to BCNF'::varchar, 'Revision notes on functional dependencies, anomalies and normal forms up to BCNF, with one table decomposed step by step.'::varchar, 'seed/normalization-1nf-bcnf.md'::varchar, 'public'::varchar, 'ananya.rao@scholarmesh.test'::varchar, 'UE23CS351A'::varchar, INTERVAL '41 days', $n$Normalization means organising tables so each fact is stored in exactly one place. It is driven by functional dependencies (FDs): X -> Y means that any two rows with the same X must have the same Y. For example student_id -> student_name, because one ID cannot have two names. Badly designed tables suffer from anomalies. An update anomaly is changing a lecturer's office in one row but not the others. An insert anomaly is being unable to add a new course until some student enrols. A delete anomaly is losing the course details when the last student drops it.

1NF requires every cell to hold one atomic value, with no lists or repeating groups, so a phones column containing '9876, 9123' must become separate rows. 2NF means the table is in 1NF and no non-key attribute depends on only part of a composite key (a partial dependency). 3NF means it is in 2NF and no non-key attribute depends on another non-key attribute (a transitive dependency, A -> B -> C). BCNF is stricter: for every non-trivial FD X -> Y, X must be a superkey.

Worked example. Take ENROLMENT(student_id, course_id, course_name, lecturer, lecturer_office) with key (student_id, course_id). The dependency course_id -> course_name, lecturer is partial because course_id is only half the key, so for 2NF we move it out into COURSE(course_id, course_name, lecturer, lecturer_office). Now lecturer -> lecturer_office is transitive, so for 3NF we split again into COURSE(course_id, course_name, lecturer) and LECTURER(lecturer, lecturer_office). What is left is ENROLMENT(student_id, course_id).

BCNF catches a case 3NF allows. In TEACHES(student, subject, teacher), where each teacher teaches one subject, teacher -> subject holds but teacher is not a superkey. Split it into (student, teacher) and (teacher, subject).$n$::text),
  ('Transactions, ACID and Isolation Levels', 'Explains ACID, the three concurrency anomalies and the SQL isolation levels using a bank transfer.', 'seed/acid-isolation-levels.md', 'public', 'rahul.verma@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS351A', INTERVAL '38 days', $n$A transaction is a group of SQL operations treated as one unit of work. The standard example is a bank transfer of 500 from account A to B: debit A, credit B. ACID is what the DBMS promises. Atomicity: all or nothing, so a crash after the debit rolls the debit back. Consistency: the database moves from one valid state to another, so constraints like balance >= 0 still hold. Isolation: concurrent transactions do not see each other's half-finished work. Durability: once COMMIT returns, the change survives a crash.

Concurrent transactions can cause three classic problems. A dirty read is reading another transaction's uncommitted write; if it rolls back, you used data that never existed. A non-repeatable read is reading the same row twice and getting different values because someone committed an update in between. A phantom is re-running a query and getting extra rows because someone committed a matching insert.

SQL defines isolation levels that trade safety for speed. READ COMMITTED (the PostgreSQL default) blocks dirty reads but still allows non-repeatable reads and phantoms. REPEATABLE READ stops non-repeatable reads, and in PostgreSQL its snapshot also hides phantoms. SERIALIZABLE behaves as if the transactions ran one after another, and the database may abort one with a serialization error that the application must retry. The weakest level, READ UNCOMMITTED, is rarely used.

Back to the transfer: A and B each have 1000, T1 moves 500 from A to B, and T2 sums both balances with two SELECTs. Under READ COMMITTED, T2 may read A (1000), then T1 commits, then read B (1500) and get 2500 instead of 2000. Under REPEATABLE READ, T2 uses one snapshot and sees both changes or neither.$n$),
  ('How B+ Tree and Hash Indexes Work', 'How B+ tree and hash indexes find rows, why range queries favour B+ trees, and what indexes cost on writes.', 'seed/bplus-tree-hash-indexes.md', 'public', 'karthik.iyer@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS351A', INTERVAL '33 days', $n$Without an index, a query such as SELECT * FROM student WHERE roll_no = 'X' must scan every row, so the cost grows with the table size. An index is a separate structure mapping column values to row locations, like the index at the back of a textbook: look up the term, get the page number, and jump straight there.

The B+ tree is the default index type in most relational databases. It is balanced, so all leaves sit at the same depth. Internal nodes hold only keys and child pointers to guide the search. Leaf nodes hold every key with a pointer to its row, and the leaves are linked in sorted order. Each node fits one disk page and holds hundreds of keys, so the fan-out is high and the tree stays short: with a fan-out of 200, three levels cover about 8 million keys, so a lookup takes roughly three page reads.

Because the leaves are linked and sorted, range queries are cheap. For WHERE marks BETWEEN 60 AND 80, the database descends once to the first leaf containing 60, then walks along the leaf chain until it passes 80. The same sorted order lets ORDER BY skip a separate sort step.

A hash index runs the key through a hash function to pick a bucket, so an equality lookup like WHERE roll_no = 'X' takes about one step on average. But hashing destroys ordering, so it cannot handle ranges, ORDER BY or prefix matches. Indexes are not free: every INSERT, UPDATE or DELETE must also update the affected indexes, and indexes take disk space. Only index columns you filter, join or sort on often.$n$),
  ('ER Modelling and Mapping to Relations', 'Entities, attributes, relationships and cardinality, plus the standard rules for turning an ER diagram into tables.', 'seed/er-model-mapping.md', 'course', 'diya.shah@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS351A', INTERVAL '30 days', $n$An ER (entity-relationship) model is a diagram of the data drawn before any tables exist. An entity is a thing we store facts about, like Student or Course. An attribute is a property of it, like name or email. A key attribute (underlined) uniquely identifies each entity, such as roll_no. Attributes can also be composite (name split into first and last), multivalued (phone numbers) or derived (age from date of birth).

A relationship links entities, such as Student ENROLS_IN Course. Cardinality says how many of each side can be linked: 1:1, 1:N (one Department has many Students, each Student belongs to one Department) or M:N (a Student takes many Courses and a Course has many Students). Participation says whether every entity must take part (total) or only some (partial). A weak entity has no full key of its own and is identified through an owner entity, for example a Dependent identified by the Employee's ID plus the dependent's name.

Mapping rules turn the diagram into tables. Each strong entity becomes a table with its key as the primary key. A multivalued attribute gets its own table holding the owner's key as a foreign key. For 1:N, put the foreign key on the N side: Student gets a dept_id column referencing Department. For M:N, create a junction table such as ENROLMENT(student_id, course_id) whose primary key is the composite of both foreign keys; relationship attributes like grade live there. For 1:1, the foreign key can go on either side. A weak entity becomes a table whose primary key is the owner's key plus its partial key, with a foreign key back to the owner.$n$),
  ('SQL Joins Cheat Sheet', 'Quick reference for INNER, LEFT, RIGHT, FULL, CROSS and self joins, the WHERE-on-outer-join trap, and GROUP BY with HAVING.', 'seed/sql-joins-cheat-sheet.md', 'public', 'tanvi.hegde@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS351A', INTERVAL '12 days', $n$A join combines rows from two tables on a condition. Tiny example: STUDENT has Asha (dept 1), Ben (dept 2) and Chitra (no dept); DEPARTMENT has 1 CSE, 2 ECE, 3 MECH. INNER JOIN keeps only matching rows: Asha-CSE and Ben-ECE. LEFT JOIN keeps every left row and fills NULL where nothing matches, so Chitra appears with a NULL department. RIGHT JOIN is the mirror image, so MECH appears with a NULL student. FULL OUTER JOIN keeps unmatched rows from both sides, giving four rows.

CROSS JOIN pairs every row of one table with every row of the other (no ON clause), so 3 students and 3 departments give 9 rows; useful for generating combinations, dangerous by accident. A self join joins a table to itself using two aliases, for example to list each employee beside their manager: SELECT e.name, m.name FROM employee e JOIN employee m ON e.manager_id = m.emp_id.

A very common mistake is filtering the outer side in WHERE. SELECT * FROM student s LEFT JOIN department d ON s.dept_id = d.dept_id WHERE d.dept_name = 'CSE' drops the unmatched student Chitra, because NULL = 'CSE' is never true, so the LEFT JOIN silently behaves like an INNER JOIN. If you want to filter the right table and still keep unmatched left rows, move the condition into ON.

GROUP BY collapses rows into one row per group so you can use aggregates like COUNT or AVG. WHERE filters rows before grouping; HAVING filters groups after aggregation. For example SELECT d.dept_name, COUNT(*) AS n FROM student s JOIN department d ON s.dept_id = d.dept_id GROUP BY d.dept_name HAVING COUNT(*) >= 2 lists only departments with at least two students.$n$),
  ('Concurrency Control: Two-Phase Locking and Deadlocks', 'Shared and exclusive locks, two-phase locking, strict 2PL, and how deadlocks are detected and resolved.', 'seed/concurrency-2pl-deadlocks.md', 'course', 'vikram.reddy@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS351A', INTERVAL '9 days', $n$Concurrency control makes interleaved transactions give the same result as some serial order, which is called serializability. The common tool is locking. A shared lock (S) lets a transaction read an item, and many transactions can hold S on it together. An exclusive lock (X) lets it write, and no other lock on that item is allowed. So S is compatible with S, but X conflicts with everything.

Two-phase locking (2PL) controls when locks are released. Each transaction has a growing phase, where it only acquires locks, then a shrinking phase, where it only releases them. After releasing one lock it may never acquire another. 2PL guarantees conflict serializability, meaning the result matches some serial order, because conflicting operations (same item, at least one write) get ordered consistently. Strict 2PL also holds all X locks until commit or abort, which prevents cascading rollbacks because nobody reads uncommitted data.

2PL has a price: deadlock. Example: T1 holds X on account A and requests B, while T2 holds X on B and requests A. Each waits for the other forever. The DBMS detects this with a wait-for graph, where each node is a transaction and an edge T1 -> T2 means T1 is waiting for a lock held by T2. A cycle in the graph means deadlock. The system then aborts a victim, usually the cheapest transaction to undo, and rolls it back so the others can continue.

A simpler alternative is a lock timeout: if a transaction waits longer than a set limit, it is aborted and retried. This is cheap but may abort transactions that were merely slow. Applications can also avoid many deadlocks by always locking items in the same order.$n$),
  ('Software Requirements: Functional vs Non-functional', 'How functional and non-functional requirements differ, how to make them testable, and how MoSCoW prioritises them.', 'seed/requirements-fr-nfr.md', 'public', 'karthik.iyer@scholarmesh.test', 'UE23CS344A', INTERVAL '5 days', $n$A requirement is a statement of what a system must do or how well it must do it. Functional requirements (FRs) describe behaviour: what the system does, the features and functions users can see. Non-functional requirements (NFRs) describe qualities and constraints: how well the system does it. A handy test is that FRs are verbs (the system shall generate a timetable) and NFRs are adjectives (the system shall be fast, secure, usable).

Take a library app. Functional: a member can search books by title, a librarian can issue a book, the system emails a reminder two days before the due date. Non-functional: search results appear within 2 seconds for 95 percent of requests, the system supports 500 concurrent users, and passwords are stored hashed. NFRs cover performance, security, reliability, usability and maintainability, and they often drive architecture decisions.

A good requirement is testable. 'The system should be fast' is useless because nobody can say whether it passed. 'The login page loads in under 2 seconds on a 4G connection' can be checked with a measurement. Good requirements are also unambiguous, atomic (one thing per statement) and traceable to a test case and a stakeholder need. Avoid vague words like fast, user-friendly, easy or flexible unless you attach a number or a condition.

Since time and budget are limited, requirements are prioritised, often with MoSCoW. Must have: the project fails without it (students can log in and upload notes). Should have: important but there is a workaround (email notifications). Could have: nice to have if time allows (dark mode). Won't have (this time): agreed to be out of scope for now, which keeps expectations clear.$n$)
) AS v(title, description, file_path, visibility, owner_email, course_code, age, content)
JOIN STUDENT s     ON s.email = v.owner_email
LEFT JOIN COURSE c ON c.course_code = v.course_code;
